1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,839
A planner moves vessel IMO-9312345 from
berth B2 to berth B7. Your service loads a
BerthAssignment, calls reassign("B7"), and
returns. You never called UPDATE. Yet the
database row changes.

2
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:31,320
That quiet write is Hibernate dirty
checking — and it is the internal you need
before lifecycle jargon makes sense. When
Hibernate loads an entity inside a
persistence context, it keeps a snapshot
of the loaded state.

3
00:00:31,439 --> 00:00:44,559
On flush — at transaction commit by
default, or earlier if you force it —
Hibernate compares the current field
values to that snapshot. Differences
become SQL. Unchanged entities produce no
update.

4
00:00:44,679 --> 00:00:57,359
That is the unit-of-work idea: you mutate
objects; the provider schedules SQL. Look
at this java for a moment. Look at this
java for a moment. On flush, Hibernate may
emit: Look at this example.

5
00:00:57,479 --> 00:01:12,159
UPDATE berth_assignments If berthCode
never changed, that update does not run.
Calling save on an already-managed entity
is mostly noise — Spring Data’s save for a
managed instance often just returns the
same instance.

6
00:01:12,279 --> 00:01:23,879
The persistence context already tracks it.
Flush modes matter when you interleave
queries. AUTO (default) flushes before
certain queries so you do not read stale
data you just changed in memory.

7
00:01:23,999 --> 00:01:31,759
COMMIT delays until commit — faster in
some batch paths, surprising if you query
mid-transaction expecting to see your own
writes.

8
00:01:31,879 --> 00:01:38,679
Understanding flush timing prevents "I
updated the berth but the next SELECT
still shows B2" bugs inside one
transaction.

9
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:51,639
The first-level cache is the same
persistence context: load BerthAssignment
id 17 twice in one transaction and you get
the same Java instance, not a second
SELECT by default. That identity guarantee
is intentional.

10
00:01:51,759 --> 00:02:05,839
The second-level cache is optional and
cross-transaction — a later lesson. Dirty
checking lives in the first level. People
believe every save call immediately hits
the database. It does not — SQL is
deferred until flush.

11
00:02:05,959 --> 00:02:13,799
Others mutate a detached assignment after
the transaction ended and expect dirty
checking to notice; there is no context
left to notice.

12
00:02:13,919 --> 00:02:27,199
A third trap is opening the SQL log,
seeing no update, and assuming Hibernate
is broken when the field simply did not
change relative to the snapshot. Dirty
checking explains *how* managed entities
write themselves.

13
00:02:27,319 --> 00:02:39,039
It does not yet name the states an entity
can be in — transient, managed, detached,
removed — or what merge does when a clerk
edits a cargo item after the request that
loaded it has ended.

14
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:41,759
That lifecycle vocabulary is next.
